WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
Brazil planted fuel on its best pasture to replace oil, and the cattle walked north into the Amazon. This is Unintended Consequences, episode 99. From a small plane over the interior of Sao Paulo State, the ground looks almost oceanic, kilometer after kilometer of sugarcane, grown less for the table than for the tank. A days travel north along the ragged arc of settlement that rings the Amazon, the view inverts. Pale rectangles of new pasture appear

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:57.700
where forests stood last year, trees burned or stacked in windrows, grass seeded for cattle that used to live much farther south. This was designed to wean Brazil off imported oil, and to give its sugar industry a domestic future. Instead, it helped set off an indirect land use cascade, cane onto pasture, soy into the gap, cattle into the trees. A celebrated climate fuel ended up tied to some of the fastest forest loss on earth.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:26.620
The energy crop was planted nowhere near the rainforest, so how did a field in the center south help clear trees a days travel north. In the mid-1970s, Brazil's economy ran on oil it did not have. The first oil shock turned a manageable import into a national emergency. Tankers and dollar bills were leaving the country. Inflation was already high. A military government under President Ernesto Geisel watched a strategic weakness become a fiscal one.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:39.420
What Brazil did have was sugar cane, a crop stitched into the coast since the colonial mills. It also had a sugar industry that had just written a brief world price boom and then watched that boom collapse.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:51.240
The National Alcohol Program, Pro Alcohol, braided those two problems into a single industrial policy, ferment cane into ethanol, blend it into gasoline or burn it neat.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:02:07.460
Cut the oil bill. Give mill owners a home market that foreign sugar buyers could not snatch away. Geisel's government was not groping in the dark. Brazil already knew how the grocane already had mills, already had a car culture that could be told what to put in the tank.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:19.500
Ethanol looked like energy and independence with rural jobs attached. A patriotic substitute for a commodity, the organization of the petroleum exporting countries, OPEC, could reprice overnight.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:31.200
Planners were not aiming at the Amazon. Cain is a crop of the center south, São Paulo and its neighbors, of good soils, a dry harvest season, and roads that already existed.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:45.560
The rainforest was a different map, a different climate, a different political problem. Substituting a field of grass in the agricultural heartland for a barrel of imported crude looked with the information on the desk like a clean swap.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:58.200
The program arrived as a stack of ordinary tools, blending mandates, subsidized credit for distilleries, guaranteed purchases, pumps that made ethanol a daily liquid rather than a laboratory curiosity.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:10.880
Through the late 1970s and into the 1980s, mills multiplied in the traditional cane belt, blending shares rose, automakers sold cars built to run on hydrodris alcohol.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:18.940
For a stretch, the results looked like the textbook promised, a large share of the light vehicle fleet was drinking cane instead of gasoline.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:28.440
The oil import needle moved, then petroleum prices fell, sugar prices occasionally recovered enough to pull cane back toward the export crystal.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:36.480
Drivers of dedicated ethanol cars found themselves stranded between empty alcohol pumps and a policy that had lost its urgency.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:47.560
The industry did not vanish, it waited. In the early 2000s, it returned in a more elegant machine, flex fuel engines that could swallow any mixture of ethanol and gasoline.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:59.560
The driver no longer had to bet on a single fuel. Oil was expensive again. Climate at politics had begun to treat biofuels as a way to decarbonize driving without waiting for a new fleet.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:07.180
Brazilian officials and millers could say, with some justice, that they had built the world's most sophisticated cane ethanol system.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:14.520
High-field yields, but gas burned for mill power, a greenhouse gas story at the tailpipe that beat gasoline.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:23.180
Environmental campaigns that had learned to fear palm oil and American corn often treated Brazilian cane as the exception that proved the rule.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.080
It grew far from the Amazon, it often grew on land that was already open.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:35.320
From Brazillia and from São Paulo, the map still looked like a success that had merely been interrupted and then improved.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:42.040
The exception was less exceptional than it looked, because land is a market, not a photograph of one field.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:51.060
As flex fuel cars took over showrooms and ethanol demand recovered, then surged, cane did what a profitable crop does. It expanded.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:59.540
It expanded overwhelmingly in the center south, and overwhelmingly on to pasture. On a single property that can look like progress,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:09.060
degraded cattle grass becoming a high yield energy crop is the definition of intensification, and mill agronomists could point to it with genuine pride.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.860
The trouble started on the next property, and the one after that.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:20.520
Ranching in Brazil is a land-hungry business with thin returns per hectare, low stocking rates on much of the extensive herd,

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:23.960
and a long habit of moving when nearby land gets deer.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:35.340
Cattle are not just meat, in a frontier economy they are a way to occupy ground, to claim it, to hold it as a store of value while grass does the slow work of justifying the title.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:48.740
When millers and cane growers bit up pasture in São Paulo, mean as Gerais and Goyas, a rational rancher sold, took the capital, and bought a much larger tract where forest was still being priced as if it were empty.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:06:00.900
In Mato Grosso, in Pará, in Rondonia, along the roads that had opened the southern and eastern Amazon, one hectare released in the cane belt did not become one spare hectare for the country.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:09.340
It became several hectares of new clearing at the forest edge, because frontier land was cheaper, and because the national herd was still growing.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:15.420
Soy entered the same cascade from the side, which is why the story is not only about alcohol.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:31.100
The soybean frontier, pushing out of Parana and Rio Grande do Sul into the Cerrado, and then toward the Amazon, was driven by export demand, by breeding that taught a temperate crop to live in the tropics, and by a world hungry for protein meal.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:40.660
Cain and soy both prefer land that is already cleared and reasonably close to silos, crushers, and mills. Both therefore preferred pasture.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:51.980
Where soy took the grass in Mato Grosso, the cows did not disappear. They moved again, another step north and west, into the places where the only cheap land left was forest.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.520
Researchers who later tried to follow this chain called it indirect land use change.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:06.180
The feedstock never had to touch the trees. The displacement did. Direct conversion of Amazon forest to sugarcane remained rare.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:17.000
Humidity and rain in the harvest season make the crop a poor fit there anyway, which is why the satellite picture of the cane belt could look so virtuous while the satellite picture of the forest did not.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:28.640
In the 2000s, as ethanol production climbed to meet a flex fuel fleet, and as soy filled trucks bound for the coast, Amazon clearing ran at some of the highest rates ever recorded.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:41.080
Newly deforested land went, by and large, to cattle pasture. The fuel in a tank in Campinas did not fell a specific tree in South Felix, Lushingu, in a way a prosecutor could photograph.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:48.700
It changed relative prices in a land market that already rewarded clearing. Second order effects piled onto the first.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:08:01.680
The Cerrado, a savanna woodland of enormous biodiversity and deep carbon stocks, absorbed much of the direct expansion of both soy and cane, and received a fraction of the Amazon's international grief.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:13.920
Millions of hectares of grass became crop land in the agricultural heartland, which raised land prices, favored larger holdings, and made it still more attractive to speculate in frontier claims.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:21.980
Labor followed the mills, hard harvest work in the cane, then increasing mechanization that cut jobs as it cut costs.

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:33.380
Water followed too, in dry pockets of the center south, where a thirsty crop met a thirsty climate. Third order, the climate arithmetic of ethanol itself became a fight.

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:40.460
Count only the mill, the tractor, and the tailpipe, and cane alcohol can look like a real improvement on gasoline.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:51.500
Count the forest that would not have been cleared if the cattle had stayed on the old grass, and a fuel adopted to reduce emissions can spend years paying back a carbon debt incurred somewhere,

00:08:51.500 --> 00:09:08.360
the mill never planted. The deepest irony was spatial, a policy designed to substitute for oil, a commodity drawn from underground, ended up rearranging a commodity produced on the surface, grass, and grass in Brazil is the leading edge of the forest.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.960
By the second half of the 2000s, the connection was no longer a rumor among agronomists.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:22.620
Brazilian and international researchers were mapping the pasture to cane and pasture to soy displacements, and asking what they implied for the forest.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:30.480
The government's response and the industries went first after the direct footprint, which was the footprint you could regulate with a map.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:37.320
Agro ecological zoning restricted where sugarcane could expand, and kept mills out of the Amazon biome.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:45.880
That was a real constraint on a distillery, and a weaker one on the rancher who sold to a mill and bought land somewhere the zoning did not reach.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:57.900
Soy traders, under campaign pressure, agreed not to buy beans grown on Amazon land cleared after a cutoff date, direct soy driven deforestation in the forest biome fell sharply.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:03.400
Cattle, the residual user of freshly-feld land, were not in that agreement.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:15.740
Later tools, enforcement against unauthorized clearing, restrictions on rural credit, a rewritten forest code, brought Amazon rates down for a time, which showed that policy could bite.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:22.520
Then those rates proved politically fragile, which showed that the underlying land market had not been repealed.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:31.820
Ethanol itself was not reversed. Flexfuel remains the everyday architecture of Brazilian driving. Cain remains a pillar of the rural economy.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:38.860
Blending mandates still exist in countries that learned from Brazil and from their own programs to worry about leakage.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:46.300
California, the European Union, and others began writing indirect land use factors into biofuel rules.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:54.260
Contested multipliers, fought over by millers and environmental economists, but an admission that the tank is connected to the frontier.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:11:04.740
The meta-irony is familiar. The cleaner the certified hectare of Cain, the more the remaining damage hides in the hectare that was never certified because it was never Cain.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:09.400
Land use leakage is what happens when you fix a field and ignore a market.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:15.300
If a policy makes one use of land more valuable, the uses it displaces do not vanish.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:21.660
They look for the next cheapest acre, and in a country with a forest frontier, that acre is often trees.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:28.560
Measuring only the thing you subsidize will flatter the intervention because the harm has been asked to relocate.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:36.780
The pattern is now the pattern of a dozen climate policies. Carbon offsets that protect one forest while logging shifts to the next.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:44.260
Renewable fuel rules that treat a crop as carbon neutral, so long as that crop did not personally bulldoze a biome.

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:54.500
Even the sighting of clean infrastructure that assumes the map is closed, that the land you did not use is simply unused, rather than claimed by whatever you pushed aside.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:12:03.300
If you are looking at a climate project, a fuel standard, or an offset contract, the thing to watch is not only the hectare in the photograph.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:12.320
Watch the next cheapest hectare. Watch whether the displaced activity had somewhere cheaper to go, and whether that somewhere still had trees on it.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:19.920
When we count the benefit in the tank, or in the credit, or on the certified hectare, whose land are we leaving out of the ledger?

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:29.820
That's the case for today. The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

